        {
          "start": "10:30",
          "end": "12:00",
          "kind": "panel",
          "title": "Panel 1: Towards Easier NDN Application Development",
          "chair": {
            "name": "Christian Tschudin",
            "affiliation": "University of Basel"
          },
          "panelists": [
            {
              "name": "Alex Afanasyev",
              "affiliation": "Florida International University"
            },
            {
              "name": "Jeff Burke",
              "affiliation": "UCLA REMAP"
            },
            {
              "name": "Kathleen Nichols",
              "affiliation": "Pollere, Inc."
            },
            {
              "name": "Davide Pesavento",
              "affiliation": "NIST"
            },
            {
              "name": "Constantin Serban",
              "affiliation": "Perspecta Labs"
            }
          ],
          "abstract": "Application developments play a fundamental role in driving NDN's architectural design and development. They help validate architectural design decisions, identify remaining issue, and drive the NDN rollout.\n\nThis panel aims to:\n1. assess the existing tools developed in recent years that promote NDN app development (comment: make people aware of what's, or soon to be, available)\n2. What still hard? identify and prioritize remaining issues, and\n3. explore new application areas that may benefit most from NDN's new architectural features as compared to TCP/IP."
        },
        {
          "start": "12:00",
          "end": "13:00",
          "kind": "break",
          "title": "Lunch"
        },
        {
          "start": "13:00",
          "end": "14:00",
          "kind": "panel",
          "title": "Panel 2: Enabling a Data-Centric Ecosystem for Big Data Applications",
          "chair": {
            "name": "Lan Wang",
            "affiliation": "University of Memphis"
          },
          "panelists": [
            {
              "name": "Nasir Ali",
              "affiliation": "University of Memphis"
            },
            {
              "name": "Christos Papadopoulos",
              "affiliation": "Colorado State University"
            },
            {
              "name": "Alex Szalay",
              "affiliation": "Johns Hopkins University"
            },
            {
              "name": "Edmund Yeh",
              "affiliation": "Northeastern University"
            }
          ],
          "abstract": "The amount of data generated by individuals and businesses have been growing exponentially. An IDC report “The Digital Universe in 2020” projects that, by 2020, there will be around 40 zettabytes of data.\n\nDomain experts have been collecting and analyzing ever growing data sets to derive insights from the data, but they face the challenges of individually building their own systems or developing their own solutions to handle big data. As of now, most people develop big data applications based on commercially available cloud services, which provide a logical central place for storage and processing but do not address some of the very fundamental problems, including:\n• unstructured data collections that are difficult to navigate;\n• lack of systematic solutions to security and privacy when data is outside the cloud, and lack of auditability when the data is inside the cloud; and\n• the need to deal with lower layer details for storing, transferring, and processing data.\n\nWe believe that harnessing the big data revolution will require a big data ecosystem that enables data sharing across the boundaries of different vendors, different providers, and different applications. Our 9-year research efforts on the design and development of Named Data Networking (NDN) project also convince us that we can develop this big data ecosystem by taking on a data-centric design approach. The goal of this panel is to\n• understand the requirements of big data application in specific domains, and\n• examine the problems from multiple diverse domains to identify potential common application patterns, common needs and requirements.\n\nThe panel will steer the discussion toward formulate a common big data ecosystem framework that supports the management of data through its life cycle, starting from data production, naming and securing data at the production time, to structured data storage and scalable retrieval, and to controlled data consumption."
        },
